00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:12.260
Life in Zimbabwe has never been easy.

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:17.390
Since its independence, the Southern African
country has seen factional violence, international

00:00:17.390 --> 00:00:20.740
sanctions, and rampant political instability.

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:26.369
But in the mid 2000’s, it lost something
even more fundamental: its currency.

00:00:26.369 --> 00:00:31.210
Surviving meant waking up as early as 2 in
the morning, trekking to the nearest ATM,

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:32.689
and then waiting in line.

00:00:32.689 --> 00:00:37.761
On a good day, you’d be able to withdraw
the equivalent of about one or two US dollars

00:00:37.761 --> 00:00:40.329
- the maximum allowed by the government.

00:00:40.329 --> 00:00:45.649
Or, you might find, after losing sleep and
waiting for hours, that no more money was

00:00:45.649 --> 00:00:46.649
left.

00:00:46.649 --> 00:00:52.489
That’s because, around 2007, Zimbabwe experienced
the second highest inflation in history, after

00:00:52.489 --> 00:00:53.990
Post-War Hungary.

00:00:53.990 --> 00:00:58.620
It’s hard to know exactly how bad it got,
as the government stopped reporting numbers

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:05.199
after 100,000% inflation, when a loaf of bread
cost 30 billion Zimbabwean dollars.

00:01:05.199 --> 00:01:09.140
Employees stopped going to work when their
annual salary wouldn’t even pay for their

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:10.630
bus ride home.

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:15.319
And vending machines were put out of service
- unable to hold the billions of coins that

00:01:15.319 --> 00:01:17.729
a single can of soda would cost.

00:01:17.729 --> 00:01:23.090
To keep up, the central bank kept printing
bigger and bigger banknotes - a million dollars,

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:29.229
one hundred billion, and, finally, one hundred
trillion - worth a whole 40 US cents.

00:01:29.229 --> 00:01:31.849
So, how did Zimbabwe get here?

00:01:31.849 --> 00:01:36.509
And how did this devastating economic crisis
help launch Africa’s financial technology

00:01:36.509 --> 00:01:40.179
revolution?

00:01:40.179 --> 00:01:44.909
Zimbabwe is home to the largest waterfall
in the world: Victoria Falls - over twice

00:01:44.909 --> 00:01:50.299
the height of Niagara, and whose tumbling
water can be heard 40 kilometers, or 25 miles

00:01:50.299 --> 00:01:51.579
away.

00:01:51.579 --> 00:01:56.579
Further South are the intricate stone ruins
of an 11th century palace city.

00:01:56.579 --> 00:02:03.399
And savannas everywhere in between hold Africa’s
Big Five: lions, leopards, elephants, buffalo,

00:02:03.399 --> 00:02:04.499
and rhinos.

00:02:04.499 --> 00:02:08.209
Unfortunately, few will ever get to see these
wonders.

00:02:08.209 --> 00:02:13.040
Because, in addition to being blessed with
natural beauty, Zimbabwe is also cursed with

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:16.900
an abundance of gold, platinum, coal, and
diamonds.

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:21.590
All things which led Cecil Rhodes of the British
South Africa Company to invade the land with

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:25.299
the newly invented Maxim Gun at the end of
the 19th century.

00:02:25.299 --> 00:02:31.519
The company-run territory was almost entirely
governed and exploited by the tiny white minority.

00:02:31.519 --> 00:02:36.909
Years later, two rival factions emerged: Zimbabwe’s
African People’s Union, supported by the

00:02:36.909 --> 00:02:41.099
Soviet Union, and the National Union, backed
by Mao’s China.

00:02:41.099 --> 00:02:46.790
In 1980, Zimbabwe gained its independence
and the National Party won its first election.

00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:51.959
Almost immediately, with the spirit of nationalism
strong, leader Robert Mugabe began doing what

00:02:51.959 --> 00:02:55.420
all good dictators do: consolidate power.

00:02:55.420 --> 00:03:00.099
His army, trained by North Korea under Kim
Il Sung, began murdering dissidents.

00:03:00.099 --> 00:03:05.290
He granted himself the power to dissolve parliament,
declare martial law, and removed all term

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:06.290
limits.

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:12.510
Mugabe’s authoritarian rule lasted 37 years,
just 4 short of Africa’s record, and teemed

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:13.939
with corruption.

00:03:13.939 --> 00:03:19.379
He once accidentally let slip that his opponent
had won 73% of the vote in the last election

00:03:19.379 --> 00:03:21.480
before quickly correcting himself.

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:25.420
This insatiable thirst for power had more
than political consequences.

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:31.090
In 2000, his government began forcibly seizing
land from white farmers for redistribution.

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:35.790
Officially, the goal was to correct for the
country’s unjust colonial past.

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:38.940
In reality, the policy help him buy political
support.

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:41.430
The effects were devastating.

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:46.959
The new farmers usually had little or no experience
and sometimes no interest in agriculture.

00:03:46.959 --> 00:03:52.900
From 2000 to 2009, total agricultural output
was cut in half, with some farms producing

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:55.260
only a tenth as much as before.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:04:00.709
By wiping out two of its largest crops: corn
and tobacco, land reform both singlehandedly

00:04:00.709 --> 00:04:04.689
destroyed its economy and decimated its food
supply.

00:04:04.689 --> 00:04:08.359
Meanwhile, many of its skilled farmers fled
for safety.

00:04:08.359 --> 00:04:14.010
As farms became less productive and demand
for the little food left rose, so did prices.

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:15.109
And fast.

00:04:15.109 --> 00:04:20.329
Daily inflation reached 98% and Zimbabwe’s
economy totally collapsed.

00:04:20.329 --> 00:04:25.400
Shops, if they were still open, increased
prices multiple times a day as 12 million

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:29.080
people struggled to find food, water, and
power.

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:32.610
Now, to slow inflation, a government must
do two things:

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:35.310
First, it has to stop printing money.

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:40.580
Basic economics says increasing money in circulation
simply decreases its value.

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:46.770
Second, and much harder is a government has
to convince people that its currency has value.

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:48.789
Mugabe did neither.

00:04:48.789 --> 00:04:53.970
To fund its involvement in the Second Congo
War, the Reserve Bank kept printing new, higher

00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:54.970
denominations.

00:04:54.970 --> 00:04:57.310
But it just couldn’t keep up.

00:04:57.310 --> 00:05:02.390
The bank spent $500,000 US Dollars a week
ordering new banknotes, which, by the time

00:05:02.390 --> 00:05:05.950
they arrived from Germany, were already worthless.

00:05:05.950 --> 00:05:12.830
Twice it redenominated - removing 10 zeros
from all banknotes in 2008 and 12 in 2009

00:05:12.830 --> 00:05:14.870
- but, to no avail.

00:05:14.870 --> 00:05:19.800
Zimbabweans didn’t believe their currency
had value, and, therefore, it didn’t.

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:24.509
In other words, prices kept rising largely
because people expected them to.

00:05:24.509 --> 00:05:26.550
And soon, no more money was left.

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:29.560
There just weren’t enough bills to go around.

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:32.379
And that’s when Zimbabwe got creative.

00:05:32.379 --> 00:05:36.740
Like much of Africa, the vast majority of
its population is unbanked.

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:41.889
In advanced economies, about 92% of people
have some kind of bank account, but that number

00:05:41.889 --> 00:05:44.900
is only 20% in sub-saharan Africa.

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:50.139
Here, where the number of transactions are
high but balances are low, branch locations

00:05:50.139 --> 00:05:57.440
and ATMs just aren’t very profitable - leaving
Zimbabwe with 6.5 ATMS per 100,000 people,

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:07.360
Uganda with 4.2, and Niger with 1.7, compared
to the United States’ 174 or Macao’s 324.

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:11.870
Zimbabweans lacked the infrastructure necessary
to keep money safe or transfer it between

00:06:11.870 --> 00:06:12.930
people.

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:16.771
Not only does this hide the flow of money
- granting cover to criminals and making it

00:06:16.771 --> 00:06:22.320
impossible for the government to regulate
or tax it, but it’s also physically dangerous.

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:27.030
Many who live in cities regularly send money
to family members in the country, requiring

00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:31.770
they take a one or two day journey themselves
or hire someone to do it for them - and risk

00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:33.240
having it stolen.

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:37.630
But while this lack of banks would ordinarily
only slow the continent’s technological

00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:41.889
progress, African entrepreneurs turned it
into an advantage.

00:06:41.889 --> 00:06:44.580
What it does have a lot of are phones.

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:49.560
In many of it countries, total mobile phone
penetration stands at 80%.

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:54.610
These two things allowed countries like Zimbabwe
to leapfrog over checks and credit cards,

00:06:54.610 --> 00:06:58.280
and then surpass more advanced economies in
mobile payments.

00:06:58.280 --> 00:07:04.680
In places like the U.S., the ubiquity of banks
and credit cards actually holds back new technologies.

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:09.539
Tapping or texting would make sending money
much easier, but first, you and I need the

00:07:09.539 --> 00:07:10.830
same app.

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:17.280
Hence why there are so many competitors - Chase
Pay, Google Pay, Square Wallet, PayPal, Apple

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:19.759
Cash, Cash App, Zelle, and Venmo.

00:07:19.759 --> 00:07:24.620
The hard part isn’t building the app, it’s
hitting a critical mass of users.

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:29.949
In Africa, the selling point was obvious:
Either take a two day bus ride, or send a

00:07:29.949 --> 00:07:30.949
text.

00:07:30.949 --> 00:07:35.639
And because many of its countries are dominated
by a telecom monopoly - that company can ensure

00:07:35.639 --> 00:07:37.780
quick and universal adoption.

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:43.900
In 2007, for example, Kenya’s largest mobile
network operator, Safaricom, launched M-PESA,

00:07:43.900 --> 00:07:47.540
’m’ for mobile and ‘pesa’, meaning
money.

00:07:47.540 --> 00:07:52.970
Each M-PESA user is associated with a SIM
card, allowing them to text anyone else money.

00:07:52.970 --> 00:07:56.979
There’s no charge to sign-up or deposit,
and fees are minimal.

00:07:56.979 --> 00:08:01.440
But what’s truly revolutionary is that it
doesn’t require a bank account.

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:06.840
To deposit or withdraw, you simply find an
agent - over 100,000 middle-men who get paid

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:12.430
to collect and then bring cash to banks, or
take out cash for when users withdraw or transfer

00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:13.430
money.

00:08:13.430 --> 00:08:18.210
It’s so convenient that it’s used for
almost everything - school fees, water, electricity,

00:08:18.210 --> 00:08:19.830
food, and so on.

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:26.930
In Zimbabwe, the preferred app is called EcoCash,
which in 2017, had 6.7 million users compared

00:08:26.930 --> 00:08:28.850
to its two million bank accounts.

00:08:28.850 --> 00:08:35.630
That year, the Reserve Bank reported digital
payments accounted for 90% of its $97.5 billion

00:08:35.630 --> 00:08:40.850
dollars in total transactions, making its
economy virtually cashless.

00:08:40.850 --> 00:08:45.590
Even digital currency, however, isn’t immune
to political incompetence.

00:08:45.590 --> 00:08:51.050
As a landlocked country, Zimbabwe relies on
its powerful rivers for water and electricity.

00:08:51.050 --> 00:08:55.940
The Kariba dam alone provides over half of
the nation’s electricity, making its frequent

00:08:55.940 --> 00:08:58.010
droughts extremely dangerous.

00:08:58.010 --> 00:09:03.050
In July, Econet generators failed to start
after a power outage, disabling its mobile

00:09:03.050 --> 00:09:07.950
payment platform, and, therefore effectively,
the national economy.

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:13.510
Lifting a nation out of poverty is so difficult
not for a lack of resources but rather because

00:09:13.510 --> 00:09:16.870
solutions require deep, political changes.

00:09:16.870 --> 00:09:20.900
To rebuild its economy, Zimbabwe must first
manage its inflation.

00:09:20.900 --> 00:09:23.800
To manage inflation, it has to build confidence.

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:27.130
And to do that, its government requires reform.

00:09:27.130 --> 00:09:34.070
That hope came in 2017, when Mugabe was deposed
after nearly 4 decades of economic ruin.

00:09:34.070 --> 00:09:39.840
Fear promptly returned when this year Zimbabwe
reintroduced its Dollar and inflation again,

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:41.260
began increasing.

00:09:41.260 --> 00:09:45.580
Yet, even while countries like Zimbabwe and
Kenya clearly still have plenty of unresolved

00:09:45.580 --> 00:09:48.710
challenges, they’re also model examples
of how those very same challenges are the

00:09:48.710 --> 00:09:51.630
necessary ingredients for innovation.

00:09:51.630 --> 00:09:55.570
Mobile money is, at best, a convenience for
most of us.

00:09:55.570 --> 00:09:58.900
But for the world without bank accounts, it’s
life-changing.

00:09:58.900 --> 00:10:04.380
By making transactions faster and more secure
- apps like M-PESA literally increase the

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:05.790
value of money.

00:10:05.790 --> 00:10:10.640
Theft is reduced, there’s less waiting in
line or time spent delivering money, credit

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:16.460
is more accessible to entrepreneurs, it increases
savings, and improves accountability.

00:10:16.460 --> 00:10:21.160
Africa is the world-leader in mobile money
precisely and only because it previously had

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:22.580
so far to come.

00:10:22.580 --> 00:10:28.010
And as the youngest continent, on the receiving
end of massive amounts of loans, its financial

00:10:28.010 --> 00:10:30.360
revolution is just the beginning.

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:36.940
What’s genius about M-PESA is how it piggybacked
on existing technology - phones were widespread,

00:10:36.940 --> 00:10:40.570
and SIM-cards and carriers were well established
in Africa.

00:10:40.570 --> 00:10:45.330
Likewise, the expensive part of cell service
- towers and equipment has already been built

00:10:45.330 --> 00:10:47.990
by big companies like T-Mobile and Sprint.

00:10:47.990 --> 00:10:52.700
Ting can help you save money by using those
exact same networks in the U.S. but without

00:10:52.700 --> 00:10:53.960
a monthly plan.

